<div class="common-acid-wrapper">
	<div class="common-acid-card">
		<div class="acid-header-row">
			<span class="acid-emoji">🧪</span>
			<h3 class="acid-title">Common Denominator: Acid (Does Stress Create Acid in the Body?)</h3>
		</div>

		<p class="acid-lead-text">
			Looking at my personal trigger list—alcohol, coffee, sparkling water, fruits, grains, vegetables, and stress—one overwhelming pattern emerges: <strong>Acid is the common environmental denominator.</strong>
		</p>

		<p class="acid-body-text">
			While coffee, wine, citrus, and carbonated beverages deliver direct, soluble hydrogen ions ($H^+$) into the oral cavity, foods like grains and vegetables feed naturally occurring oral bacteria that ferment starches into lactic acid. But what about <strong>stress</strong>? Does psychological stress actually create acid in the human body?
		</p>

		<p class="acid-highlight-title">
			<strong>Yes—in three distinct biological ways:</strong>
		</p>

		<ol class="acid-list">
			<li>
				<strong>Salivary Buffering Shutdown:</strong> Acute and chronic stress activate the sympathetic nervous system ("fight or flight"), suppressing parasympathetic saliva production. This reduces total salivary flow and severely lowers <em>bicarbonate ($HCO_3^-$) buffer concentrations</em>. Without alkaline saliva to wash away and neutralize acids, resting mouth pH drops into an acidic flare zone.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Systemic Metabolic Acidosis & Cortisol:</strong> Elevated cortisol and adrenaline alter cellular metabolism, driving higher rates of anaerobic glycolysis and systemic lactic acid production. Chronic stress also impairs kidney bicarbonate reabsorption, pushing bodily fluids toward low-grade metabolic acidosis.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Silent Gastric Acid Surges & Reflux:</strong> Stress increases stomach acid production while relaxing the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). Micro-droplets of gaseous hydrochloric acid ($HCl$) creep up into the pharynx and oral cavity during sleep or speech, bathing delicate tongue mucosa in micro-concentrations of gastric acid.
			</li>
		</ol>

		<p class="acid-summary-text">
			Whether introduced directly from a coffee cup, generated via bacterial sugar fermentation, or triggered internally by stress-induced salivary shutdown, <strong>acid is the chemical key that unlocks ASIC3 pain receptors on sensitized oral nerves.</strong>
		</p>
	</div>
